{
  "seam": "C3",
  "name": "reattach-non-pty",
  "mark": {
    "published": "e1"
  },
  "kind": "sequence",
  "grade": "reference",
  "graded_by": [
    {
      "unit": "U3",
      "test": "worker/tests/test_reattach.py — a non-PTY session's handshake carries no buffer and no gap: the worker stops reading the channel while no core peer holds its data socket"
    }
  ],
  "description": "Only PTY streams get the worker-side ring (Contract §15.1, binding; session model §4). A non-PTY session — here an SFTP subsystem — runs the same handshake, which gates its input and resumes its output, but while no core peer holds its data socket the worker stops reading that channel (SSH flow control holds the target), so it reads nothing no core can receive, and nothing is replayed: after a re-dial its hello_ack says first_buffered_seq = current_seq + 1, and no gap frame follows. Output already sent into a peer that died — 41 to 43 in the second re-dial — is the transfer layer's: a transfer resumes by offset at its own layer (Contract §15.1: never replayed through a buffer), outside the recording contract and with no gap frame (orchestrator DEFAULT, approval page). That rule is sftp's alone: exec, netconf and forward have no loss rule at this tag, and the tag that first opens one says how its loss is declared. The replay is complete at once — no gap frame is due and no buffered frame is at or after from_seq — so the handshake never waits for a frame that will not come. Published at e1 only: no v0.2 worker opens a non-PTY session (SFTP and NETCONF answer not_supported until a later release defines the file surface); it is required green with the tag that opens one.",
  "corpus": [
    "Contract §15.1",
    "session model §4",
    "immortality §6.1",
    "decision 5.43"
  ],
  "case": {
    "socket": "conn-control",
    "messages": [
